Tried a wizard while watching something mindless last night. I even got
down a few levels before discovering he only had an intelligence of 
13 (!).  I kept going, in spite of impending doom.  

A monster was generated behind the boulder I was pushing into positon on
the first level of sokoban.  Worried that it could be a co-aligned
unicorn (which I wouldn't be able to handle, even without the
penalties), I wewnt to the mines.


By minetown, I'd left off spell-casting for a bit,and was wearing
dwarvish mithril.  Mmm, a co-aligned altar, and a few convenient 
gnomes & dwarves.  And a brown pudding.

Annoying things, puddings.  It bit and rotted something while I was
fiting a dwarf.  So I poked it a couple of times, led the heard on a
chase, and started poking them to appear in the temple.

They were hard to avoid killing.  I even dulled a dagger (unfortunately,
I had no knives).  I even had many times when I'd poke one, and it would
neither split nor die.  I also had to stand in front of the door to keep
them from fleeing.  All in all, farming brown puddings at an attended
altar is a pain in the neck.  

I got the bright idea to leave, but that pack of elves with bows made it
kind of tough.  They also kept shooting the puddings in the process.  I
went back in, figuring I'd have to toughen up from agriculture. After
dancing around a couple of times to get the priest away from the altar,
I ate the pudding I'd killed in my normal space.  

Whoops.  It had stacked with an older one I'd forgotten about.  Also,
I'd just prayed.

I had two choices here: try to sacrifice into good graces in time, or
blindly drink potions.  I chose the sacrifice route.  And failed.  As it
turned out, one of the potions was full healing--but it was later in the
list than the paralysis, and there was something else nasty in the list,
too.

sigh.

>From now on, I'm only bothering farming black puddings, and not messing
with temples . . .

